+### [SplatXR On-Headset 3D Gaussian Splatting Renderer](18166-splatxr-on-headset-3d-gaussian-splatting-renderer.md)
+SplatXR is a Unity-based renderer designed to display 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) assets directly on Extended Reality (XR) headsets, supporting both in-editor previews and on-headset execution with automatic camera switching. It distinguishes itself from general-purpose GPU-sorted renderers by offloading the depth-sorting of splats to a background CPU thread at a configurable rate, allowing the GPU shader to focus solely on rasterization and alpha-blending. This approach trades off sorting latency for reduced GPU overhead, making it suitable for mobile XR hardware where computational budgets are shared with other system tasks.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
--   **Time Complexity**: Sorting dominates [G].
--   **Memory Complexity**: $O(N)$ for positions, keys, values, and histograms [G].
--   **Measured Performance**: Not given in the sources.
--   **Scaling**: Scales well with $N$ on GPU. CPU sort scales with CPU cores but is bottlenecked by memory bandwidth [G].
--   **Strengths**: Correct depth ordering for alpha blending; VR support; optional full SH rendering; flexible sorting backend [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial].
--   **Limitations**: Requires GPU for fast sorting (or falls back to slower CPU sort); 32-bit sort may be slightly slower than 24-bit but avoids artifacts; instanced rendering may have overhead for very small numbers of splats [S1, unofficial].
--   **When to Use**: When correct depth ordering is critical for alpha blending in 3DGS scenes, and VR or full SH rendering is desired [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial].
--   **When Not to Use**: When performance is paramount and approximate sorting (e.g., screen-space order) is acceptable, or when hardware lacks GPU subgroup support and CPU sort is too slow [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Sorting is $O(N \log N)$ or $O(N)$ depending on the radix sort implementation (GPU multi-radix or CPU radix). Rendering is $O(N)$ for instanced drawing.
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N)$ for storing positions, keys, values, and histograms.
+*   **Measured Performance:** Not given in the sources.
+*   **Scaling:** Not given in the sources.
+*   **Strengths:** Reduces GPU overhead by offloading sorting to CPU, suitable for mobile XR hardware [S1, unofficial]. Stable within-batch alpha-blend order prevents splat popping during slow camera orbits [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** 24-bit radix sort has artifacts on some datasets, so 32-bit sort is used by default [S1, unofficial].
+*   **When to use:** Mobile XR hardware, real-time on-device rendering [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial].
+*   **When not to use:** When GPU sorting is preferred or hardware does not support CPU offloading [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-Splatapult was created by Anthony J. Thibault and released as an open-source program to display 3D Gaussian Splatting files (PLY) [S1, unofficial]. It is based on the implementation of Intel's Embree radix sort, adapted for Vulkan/OpenGL compute shaders [S1, unofficial]. The project includes build scripts for CMake and vcpkg, and supports various rendering options like sRGB and full SH [S1, unofficial]. It is used in Android, Apple, Web, Flutter, and React Native SDKs for real-time on-device Gaussian Splatting rendering [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Origin:** Splatapult is a program to display 3D Gaussian Splatting files [S1, unofficial].
+*   **History:** Developed by Anthony J. Thibault [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Usage:** Used in Android, Apple, Web, Flutter, and React Native SDKs for real-time on-device Gaussian Splatting rendering [S2, unofficial]. Supports radiance-field captures produced by Scaniverse, Polycam, Luma, and INRIA-style trainers [S2, unofficial].
 
